// Generated by tools/build_site.py from content/free-questions — free practice questions.
(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["v-d"] = {"qs": [["Concept and Role of a Mutual Fund", "How many risk levels does the SIF risk-band use?", ["Six, the same as the mutual fund riskometer used for regular schemes", "Three", "Ten", "Five"], 3, "SIF strategies carry a risk-band with five levels (Level 1 lowest to Level 5 highest), distinct from the six-level riskometer used for regular mutual fund schemes."], ["Scheme Related Information", "A regular mutual fund scheme's riskometer must be reviewed:", ["Monthly", "Only once at the time of the new fund offer, and never again", "Every five years", "Only when SEBI asks"], 0, "Riskometers are evaluated monthly based on the portfolio, and changes are disclosed so investors know if the scheme's risk has changed."], ["Scheme Related Information", "The Key Information Memorandum (KIM) is best described as:", ["A detailed legal document containing the full trust deed and AMC agreements", "A monthly portfolio statement", "An audit report", "An abridged SID that accompanies forms"], 3, "The KIM summarises the key features of a scheme, such as objective, asset allocation, loads and risk, and must be provided with every application form."], ["Interest Rate Derivatives", "In an interest rate swap, the party that pays fixed and receives floating benefits when:", ["Interest rates fall, reducing the floating amount received under the swap", "Interest rates rise", "Rates stay fixed", "The notional is repaid"], 1, "The fixed payer receives a floating rate. When rates rise, floating receipts increase while fixed payments stay the same, so the swap gains value."], ["Risk, Return and Performance of Funds", "A long-short manager buys stock A and shorts stock B. The market rises, A falls and B rises. The likely result is:", ["Gains on both sides, since a long-short strategy is hedged against all market moves", "Gain on A only", "No change in NAV", "Losses on both sides"], 3, "The long position in A loses as A falls, and the short in B loses as B rises. Long-short strategies can lose on both legs when stock selection is wrong."], ["Scheme Related Information", "The document containing fund-house-level information common to all schemes, such as details of the sponsor and trustees, is the:", ["SAI", "Key Information Memorandum given along with each application form", "Factsheet", "ISID"], 0, "The Statement of Additional Information holds statutory details common to the fund house. Scheme or strategy-specific details are in the SID or ISID."], ["Equity Futures and Forwards", "Which statement about stock futures in India is correct?", ["They are physically settled at expiry", "They are always cash-settled with no delivery of shares in any case", "They have no lot size", "They trade only OTC"], 0, "Stock futures and stock options in India are physically settled at expiry, so open positions result in delivery of shares. Index derivatives are cash-settled."], ["Risk, Return and Performance of Funds", "A fund's beta is 0.6 relative to Nifty. If Nifty falls 10%, the fund is expected to fall about:", ["16%, since beta adds to the market fall instead of scaling it", "10%", "6%", "0.6%"], 2, "Expected move = beta × market move = 0.6 × −10% = −6%. A beta below 1 indicates lower sensitivity to market movements."], ["Investment Landscape", "₹10,00,000 grows at 12% a year for 3 years, compounded annually. The ending value is closest to:", ["₹14,04,928", "₹13,60,000", "₹14,40,000", "₹13,12,000"], 0, "Value = 10,00,000 × 1.12³ = ₹14,04,928. ₹13,60,000 is simple interest for three years, which ignores compounding."], ["Legal Structure of Mutual Funds", "An AMC's board has 8 directors. Under SEBI norms, the minimum number who must be independent of the sponsor is:", ["4", "2", "6, plus the chairperson, in every case", "None"], 0, "SEBI requires at least 50% of an AMC's directors to be independent of the sponsor and associates. For the trustee board the requirement is two-thirds."], ["Interest Rates and Fixed Income Market", "A 2-year bond with face value ₹100 pays a 10% annual coupon. If the yield is 8%, its price is closest to:", ["₹103.57", "₹100.00, because the bond always trades at face value until it matures", "₹96.53", "₹104.00"], 0, "Price = 10/1.08 + 110/1.08² = 9.26 + 94.31 = ₹103.57. The bond trades at a premium because its coupon exceeds the market yield."], ["Taxation", "A non-resident investor redeems equity fund units at a gain. Tax on the gain is generally:", ["Deducted at source by the fund", "Collected only when the investor files a return in the country of residence", "Never payable in India", "Paid by the distributor"], 0, "For non-residents, the mutual fund deducts tax at source on capital gains at the applicable rates before paying redemption proceeds, subject to any treaty benefit claimed."], ["NAV, TER and Pricing of Units", "How are exchange-traded futures held by a scheme valued for NAV purposes?", ["At the original contract price until expiry, without any daily adjustment", "At the face value of the underlying", "At the fund manager's estimate", "At the exchange settlement price"], 3, "Exchange-traded derivatives are valued at the daily settlement or closing price published by the exchange, so gains and losses are reflected in NAV every day."], ["Equity Futures and Forwards", "If the futures price is well above its fair value, an arbitrageur can earn a profit by:", ["Selling spot and buying futures, then waiting for the next dividend", "Buying both spot and futures", "Buying spot and selling futures", "Selling both"], 2, "Cash-and-carry: buy the underlying, sell the overpriced futures and hold till expiry; convergence locks in the excess over carry cost."], ["Taxation", "An SIF strategy keeps at least 65% of its assets in domestic listed equity, including hedged positions as permitted by tax rules. For tax purposes it would be treated as:", ["Equity-oriented", "A specified debt-oriented fund taxed at slab rates, irrespective of holding period", "A business trust", "An exempt instrument"], 0, "Tax treatment follows the portfolio composition. A fund meeting the 65% domestic equity threshold is equity-oriented, so equity capital gains rates and the 12-month holding period apply."], ["Concept and Role of a Mutual Fund", "Which statement about short positions in an SIF is correct?", ["Unhedged shorts via derivatives are capped", "SIFs may short-sell any amount of stock without limits through the cash market", "SIFs cannot use derivatives at all", "Short positions are allowed only in debt SIFs"], 0, "SIFs may take unhedged short exposure through derivatives within a set cap (25% of net assets). Derivatives for hedging and rebalancing are permitted in addition."], ["Scheme Related Information", "An investor wants to know how often an SIF strategy allows redemptions and what notice period applies. The best source is the:", ["Consolidated Account Statement issued by the depository for the previous month", "ISID", "AMC's balance sheet", "Distributor's visiting card"], 1, "Redemption frequency and notice period are strategy terms, set out in the Investment Strategy Information Document. They can differ across SIF strategies."], ["Exchange Traded Interest Rate Futures", "A ₹5 crore bond portfolio has a modified duration of 5. A futures contract is worth ₹2 lakh with a modified duration of 6.25. How many contracts hedge the portfolio?", ["250, ignoring the different durations of the portfolio and futures", "160", "200", "125"], 2, "Contracts = (5 crore × 5) / (2 lakh × 6.25) = 25 crore / 12.5 lakh = 200. Ignoring durations gives 250."], ["Legal and Regulatory Framework", "Which body issues the certification that a distributor must pass before selling SIF strategies?", ["AMFI, through a separate SIF distributor licence that replaces the ARN", "RBI", "NISM", "CDSL"], 2, "NISM conducts the Series V-D certification for SIF distributors. Distributors also hold an ARN from AMFI, but the specific certification comes from NISM."], ["Basics of Derivatives", "A swap is best described as an agreement to:", ["Buy a share at a fixed price on a stock exchange at the end of the trading day", "Exchange cash flows in future", "Insure a portfolio against all losses", "Lend money without interest"], 1, "In a swap, two parties exchange streams of cash flows, such as fixed for floating interest, according to agreed terms over a period."], ["Investment Landscape", "A portfolio drifts from its target 50:50 equity–debt mix to 65:35 after a rally. Restoring the 50:50 mix by selling equity is called:", ["Rebalancing", "Market timing based on a forecast of the next rally", "Averaging down", "Tactical overweighting"], 0, "Rebalancing brings the portfolio back to its strategic mix, which controls risk and enforces 'sell high, buy low' discipline. It is rule-based rather than forecast-based."], ["Legal Structure of Mutual Funds", "Under SEBI regulations, a mutual fund is constituted in the form of a:", ["Private limited company owned by the investors of all schemes", "Partnership firm", "Co-operative society", "Trust"], 3, "Indian mutual funds are set up as trusts under the Indian Trusts Act, with the sponsor as settlor, the trustees holding property for unitholders and the AMC as manager."], ["Strategies Using Interest Rate Futures and Options", "A corporate treasurer will borrow in three months at a floating rate and wants protection from higher rates while keeping benefit from lower rates. The best instrument is:", ["Selling an interest rate floor, which earns premium but offers no protection", "An interest rate cap", "Buying IRF", "A receive-fixed swap"], 1, "A cap limits the borrowing rate if rates rise, while the borrower still benefits if rates fall. Buying futures or receiving fixed would gain only if rates fell."], ["Taxation", "For equity units acquired before 1 February 2018, grandfathering means that:", ["All gains on those units are fully exempt from tax forever, whenever they are sold", "Gains till 31 Jan 2018 are protected", "Indexation applies from 2001", "The units are taxed at slab rates"], 1, "Cost is stepped up to the fair market value on 31 January 2018 (subject to limits), so gains accrued up to that date are not taxed under the long-term equity regime."], ["Interest Rates and Fixed Income Market", "Which bond's price is most sensitive to a change in yields?", ["A 1-year Treasury bill purchased at the latest weekly auction", "3-year high-coupon bond", "Floating rate bond", "30-year low-coupon bond"], 3, "Long maturity and low coupons both increase duration. A 30-year low-coupon bond therefore has the largest price change for a given yield change."], ["Legal and Regulatory Framework", "SEBI's power to regulate mutual funds and SIFs flows from the:", ["Banking Regulation Act, 1949", "SEBI Act, 1992", "Indian Contract Act, 1872, and the Indian Trusts Act read together", "RBI Act, 1934"], 1, "The SEBI Act empowers SEBI to regulate collective investment vehicles. The SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations, 1996 and SIF circulars are issued under it."], ["Strategies Using Interest Rate Futures and Options", "A trader buys the near-month IRF and sells the far-month IRF of the same underlying. This is a:", ["Butterfly spread, involving three different maturities", "Basis trade", "Collar", "Calendar spread"], 3, "A calendar spread uses two expiries of the same contract. Margins are usually lower because the legs offset most price risk."], ["Strategies Using Interest Rate Futures and Options", "A trader sold 20 IRF contracts (₹2 lakh each, quoted per ₹100) at 98.60 and bought them back at 99.10. The result is:", ["Gain of ₹20,000, as the trader closed the position at a higher price", "Loss of ₹20,000", "Loss of ₹2,000", "Loss of ₹2,00,000"], 1, "Loss = 20 × 2,00,000 × (99.10 − 98.60) / 100 = ₹20,000. A short position loses when the futures price rises."], ["Risk, Return and Performance of Funds", "Fund P returned 16% with standard deviation 20%; Fund Q returned 12% with standard deviation 10%. The risk-free rate is 6%. Which has the better Sharpe ratio?", ["Fund P, because it delivered the higher absolute return over the year", "Fund Q", "Both are equal", "Cannot be computed"], 1, "Sharpe P = (16 − 6) / 20 = 0.50. Sharpe Q = (12 − 6) / 10 = 0.60. Fund Q earns more excess return per unit of total risk."], ["Investment Landscape", "An investor's risk profile questionnaire shows high willingness to take risk, but she has little savings and needs the money in a year. A distributor should give more weight to her:", ["High stated risk appetite and her strong wish to earn equity-like returns", "Preference for recent top performers", "Interest in new fund offers", "Limited risk capacity"], 3, "Risk capacity, the ability to absorb losses, limits what is suitable regardless of willingness. A short horizon and small savings mean she cannot afford large losses."], ["Legal Structure of Mutual Funds", "A Specialized Investment Fund is launched and managed by:", ["A portfolio manager registered under the SEBI PMS Regulations, separately from any mutual fund", "A bank's treasury department", "An insurance company", "An eligible mutual fund AMC"], 3, "SIFs are set up under the SEBI mutual fund framework by AMCs that meet the eligibility conditions, and are overseen by the mutual fund's trustees."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Performance", "Why should a long-short SIF be judged partly on its behaviour in falling markets?", ["Because SEBI requires SIFs to show positive returns in every quarter", "Because SIF NAVs do not fall", "Its aim is to limit downside", "Because falling markets have no effect on long-only funds"], 2, "A key promise of long-short strategies is better downside management through short positions. Checking drawdowns in falling markets tests whether this works."], ["Fund Distribution and Channel Management", "A mutual fund distributor wants to distribute SIF strategies. In addition to holding a valid ARN, the distributor must:", ["Register with RBI as an NBFC and maintain a minimum net worth", "Become a SEBI-registered portfolio manager", "Pass NISM Series V-D", "Hold a CA qualification"], 2, "SEBI requires distributors of SIF strategies to clear the NISM-Series-V-D certification, which tests knowledge of SIF strategies and derivatives, in addition to the regular MFD requirements."], ["Investor Services", "SIP, STP and SWP facilities in SIF strategies can be used by an investor:", ["Freely from the first instalment, without any minimum amount at all", "Only in debt strategies", "After the minimum is met", "Never"], 2, "SIFs allow systematic plans, but the investor must first meet the ₹10 lakh threshold at PAN level (unless exempt as an accredited investor)."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Performance", "Under two-tier benchmarking, the second-tier benchmark of a scheme:", ["Must always be the Nifty 50 Price Return Index, for every category", "Reflects the scheme's own style", "Is chosen by the investor", "Applies only to debt schemes"], 1, "The first tier reflects the category; the second tier is chosen by the AMC to better match the specific scheme's investment style or strategy."], ["Concept and Role of a Mutual Fund", "Under the SIF framework, a Sector Rotation Long-Short strategy invests at least 80% of its assets in:", ["Equity of at most four sectors", "Equity spread across at least ten sectors in equal weights", "Debt of a single sector", "Any asset class freely"], 0, "Sector rotation SIFs concentrate at least 80% in equity and equity-related instruments of a maximum of four sectors, rotating among them, with limited short exposure."], ["Concept and Role of a Mutual Fund", "An AMC already offers one Equity Long-Short SIF strategy. It wants to launch a second Equity Long-Short strategy. Under SEBI norms, this is:", ["Permitted, provided both strategies have a different fund manager and benchmark", "Not permitted", "Permitted only for accredited investors", "Mandatory once AUM crosses ₹1,000 crore"], 1, "As with mutual fund categories, an AMC may offer only one strategy in each SIF strategy type, so investors are not confused by near-identical offerings."], ["Equity Futures and Forwards", "When futures prices are above spot prices, the market is said to be in:", ["Backwardation, which reflects a positive cost of carry in the futures", "Equilibrium", "Contango", "Default"], 2, "Contango means futures trade above spot, typical when financing cost exceeds income from the asset. Backwardation is futures below spot."], ["Investment Landscape", "Which asset class typically provides regular income with lower volatility than equity?", ["Small cap equity", "High-quality debt", "Sectoral equity funds investing in a single cyclical sector such as metals", "Equity derivatives"], 1, "High-quality bonds pay regular interest and their prices move far less than equities. Small caps, sector funds and derivatives carry much higher volatility."], ["Interest Rates and Fixed Income Market", "Government of India dated securities are issued by:", ["SEBI, through the stock exchanges' primary market bidding platforms", "Commercial banks", "AMFI", "RBI on behalf of the Government"], 3, "RBI manages public debt and issues G-secs through auctions on behalf of the Government of India."], ["Understanding Index", "Impact cost is used as a criterion for index inclusion because it indicates a stock's:", ["Long-term profitability and its dividend payout history", "Credit rating", "Face value", "Liquidity"], 3, "Low impact cost means large orders can be executed with little price movement. Index providers prefer liquid stocks so the index can be tracked and traded."], ["Investor Services", "Which document is mandatory for a resident individual's KYC for mutual fund investments above micro limits?", ["PAN", "Passport, as the only acceptable identity and address proof for KYC", "Voter ID only", "Driving licence only"], 0, "PAN is mandatory for all mutual fund investments except PAN-exempt micro investments. Other documents serve as proof of address."], ["Scheme Related Information", "Which of the following must be disclosed by an SIF strategy in its offer document?", ["The personal contact numbers of all the investors who have invested in the strategy", "A guaranteed return", "Its risk-band level", "The fund manager's salary"], 2, "The ISID must show the strategy's risk-band so investors can judge its risk. Investor details are confidential and returns cannot be guaranteed."], ["Equity Options", "Theta of an option measures the change in premium due to:", ["Changes in implied volatility of the underlying over the life of the option", "Passage of time", "Changes in interest rates", "Changes in strike price"], 1, "Theta measures time decay. Options lose time value as expiry approaches, which hurts buyers and benefits writers, all else equal."], ["Legal Structure of Mutual Funds", "The custodian of a mutual fund scheme is responsible for:", ["Safekeeping securities and settling trades", "Choosing which securities the fund manager should buy and sell for the portfolio", "Selling units to investors", "Fixing the scheme's TER"], 0, "Custodians hold securities in safe custody, settle purchases and sales, and track corporate actions. Investment decisions remain with the AMC."], ["Exchange Traded Interest Rate Options", "Why must writers of exchange-traded interest rate options post margin?", ["Because they pay the premium to the buyer at the start of the contract", "Because they cannot close positions", "Their potential losses are large", "Margins are optional"], 2, "Writers receive a premium but face potentially large losses if rates move against them. Margins protect the clearing corporation against default."], ["Concept and Role of a Mutual Fund", "A Debt Long-Short SIF is most likely to use which instruments for its short exposure?", ["Short sale of equity shares in the cash market", "Gold futures only", "Exchange-traded interest rate derivatives", "Bank fixed deposits placed with the AMC's sponsor"], 2, "Debt long-short strategies take long positions in debt instruments and short exposure through interest rate derivatives, aiming to benefit from views on rates and spreads."], ["Investor Services", "Redemption proceeds of most open-ended schemes must be transferred to investors within:", ["10 working days from the end of the month in which the request is made", "3 working days", "1 month", "1 working day"], 1, "SEBI requires redemption proceeds to be paid within three working days for most schemes, with specified exceptions for schemes facing special circumstances."], ["Equity Futures and Forwards", "Daily mark-to-market settlement in futures ensures that:", ["Profits are paid only at expiry, along with the final settlement", "Gains and losses are settled daily", "The futures price never changes", "No margin is required"], 1, "MTM settles each day's gain or loss in cash, preventing large unpaid losses from building up and reducing default risk."], ["Fund Distribution and Channel Management", "Which unique code identifies the specific employee of a distributing entity who dealt with the investor?", ["ARN of the AMC's branch office where the form is submitted", "PAN of the distributor", "ISIN of the scheme", "EUIN"], 3, "The EUIN (Employee Unique Identification Number) tracks which employee or relationship manager interacted with the investor, improving accountability for advice."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Selection", "An investor with a 2-year goal and low risk tolerance asks to invest in a hybrid long-short SIF. The most suitable response is:", ["Recommend a lower-risk debt option", "Accept immediately, since hybrid strategies are always low risk", "Ask the investor to borrow to meet the minimum", "Suggest an equity long-short strategy instead"], 0, "A short horizon and low risk tolerance point towards low-duration debt or similar products. SIF strategies, even hybrid ones, may be too risky and less liquid for this goal."], ["Fund Distribution and Channel Management", "A distributor wants to advise clients for a fee and also earn commission on the same clients. Under SEBI norms:", ["Advice and distribution must be segregated", "Both can be done freely for the same client by the same individual", "Only fee income is taxable", "AMFI must approve each client"], 0, "SEBI requires a clear separation between advisory (fee from client) and distribution (commission from AMC) to avoid conflicts. The same person cannot do both for the same client."], ["Legal Structure of Mutual Funds", "Under one eligibility route, an AMC may launch SIFs if it has been operating for at least three years with average AUM over the preceding three years of at least:", ["₹500 crore across all its schemes taken together", "₹10,000 crore", "₹1,00,000 crore", "₹50 crore"], 1, "SEBI's first eligibility route requires a track record of at least three years and average AUM of at least ₹10,000 crore in the preceding three years, with no regulatory action in that period."], ["Basics of Derivatives", "A trader who takes a futures position to profit from an expected price move, without any underlying exposure, is a:", ["Hedger, because every futures position automatically reduces existing risk", "Speculator", "Arbitrageur", "Market regulator"], 1, "Speculators take on risk to profit from price views. Hedgers reduce existing risk, and arbitrageurs exploit price differences across markets."], ["Interest Rate Derivatives", "Which of these is NOT an interest rate derivative?", ["An overnight indexed swap linked to the overnight MIBOR benchmark", "A forward rate agreement", "Nifty 50 futures", "A 10-year G-sec future"], 2, "Nifty futures derive value from an equity index. OIS, FRAs and bond futures all derive value from interest rates or interest-bearing securities."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Performance", "₹10 lakh invested grew to ₹16 lakh in 4 years. The CAGR is closest to:", ["12.5%", "15.0%, being the 60% gain spread equally over the four years", "16.0%", "10.0%"], 0, "CAGR = (16/10)^(1/4) − 1 ≈ 12.47%. Dividing the 60% absolute gain by four years gives 15%, which ignores compounding."], ["Strategies Using Equity Futures and Options", "A long-short manager wants to profit from expected outperformance of bank stocks over IT stocks, with little exposure to the overall market. A suitable approach is to:", ["Buy bank futures and sell IT futures", "Buy both bank and IT futures in equal value to double the exposure to the market", "Sell Nifty futures only", "Buy put options on both sectors"], 0, "A pair trade goes long the expected outperformer and short the expected underperformer in similar amounts, so market moves largely cancel and relative performance drives returns."], ["Interest Rates and Fixed Income Market", "A normal (upward-sloping) yield curve indicates that:", ["Long-term yields exceed short-term yields", "Short-term yields are higher than long-term yields across all maturities", "All yields are equal", "Bonds have no credit risk"], 0, "Investors usually demand higher yields for lending longer, so the normal curve slopes upward. An inverted curve shows the opposite."], ["Investor Services", "When the sole holder of a folio dies without a nominee, the legal heir will typically need to submit:", ["Only a self-attested copy of the heir's PAN and nothing else", "A fresh application for allotment", "A redemption request signed by a distributor", "Legal documents proving succession"], 3, "Without a nominee, the AMC needs documents such as a succession certificate, probate, or indemnity and NOCs (based on value) to establish the claimant's right."], ["Basics of Derivatives", "Leverage in futures arises because:", ["The full contract value must be paid on the trade date in cash", "Futures never lose value", "Futures are guaranteed by SEBI", "Only a margin is paid"], 3, "A futures position needs only an initial margin, a fraction of the contract value. Small price moves therefore create large percentage gains or losses on the margin."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Performance", "A scheme beat its benchmark by an average 1.5% a year with a tracking error of 5%. Its information ratio is:", ["3.33, being the tracking error divided by the excess return earned", "0.30", "7.5", "1.50"], 1, "Information ratio = excess return / tracking error = 1.5 / 5 = 0.30. It measures how consistently a manager adds value relative to the benchmark."], ["Strategies Using Interest Rate Futures and Options", "A bond fund hedged with short IRF sees yields fall sharply. The overall result is most likely:", ["Bond gains offset by futures loss", "Gains on both the bonds and the short futures position, doubling the return", "Losses on both", "No effect on the portfolio"], 0, "Falling yields raise bond prices but also raise futures prices, so the short futures lose. A hedge gives up upside in exchange for protection."], ["Concept and Role of a Mutual Fund", "An SIF's Equity Ex-Top 100 Long-Short strategy must invest at least 65% of its assets in:", ["Large cap stocks ranked 1 to 100 by full market capitalisation", "Government securities", "Equity outside the top 100 stocks", "Units of other SIFs"], 2, "As its name suggests, this strategy focuses on companies outside the top 100 by market capitalisation, with at least 65% in such equities, while using limited short positions through derivatives."], ["Fund Distribution and Channel Management", "Commission paid to mutual fund distributors under current SEBI rules is:", ["Paid fully upfront at the time of investment out of the AMC's own books", "Trail-based only", "Paid by SEBI", "Deducted from investors' redemption"], 1, "Upfront commissions are not allowed. Distributors receive trail commission out of the scheme's expenses for as long as the client stays invested."], ["NAV, TER and Pricing of Units", "A long-short strategy has long holdings worth ₹520 crore, a short futures position with a mark-to-market loss of ₹8 crore, cash of ₹15 crore and other liabilities of ₹7 crore. With 50 crore units, the NAV is:", ["₹10.70, since futures losses are booked only when the position is finally closed", "₹10.56", "₹10.24", "₹10.40"], 3, "Net assets = 520 − 8 + 15 − 7 = ₹520 crore. NAV = 520 / 50 = ₹10.40. Daily mark-to-market losses on derivatives reduce NAV immediately; ignoring the ₹8 crore loss gives ₹10.56."], ["Legal and Regulatory Framework", "If an investor's grievance is not resolved by the AMC, the next escalation is usually:", ["Filing a police complaint against the distributor's branch office", "SEBI's SCORES platform", "The Banking Ombudsman", "AMFI's board of directors"], 1, "Investors first approach the AMC; if unresolved, they can file on SCORES. Disputes still pending can be taken to the online dispute resolution mechanism."], ["Exchange Traded Interest Rate Futures", "IRF contracts on Indian exchanges are currently mostly settled:", ["By physical delivery of the cheapest-to-deliver bond from a basket", "In gold", "In cash", "By rolling over automatically"], 2, "Exchange-traded IRFs in India are cash-settled against a final settlement price derived from the underlying security's market prices."], ["Strategies Using Interest Rate Futures and Options", "A debt manager expecting a sharp RBI rate cut wants to increase portfolio duration quickly. He should:", ["Buy IRF", "Sell IRF to reduce the impact of any rate change on the portfolio", "Buy floaters", "Shift to cash"], 0, "Long futures add duration, increasing gains if yields fall after the rate cut. Selling futures or moving to cash and floaters would reduce duration."], ["Fund Distribution and Channel Management", "Why do AMCs conduct due diligence on large distributors?", ["To fix the NAV of the schemes distributed by them on each business day", "To decide the investor's tax rate", "To appoint them as trustees", "To ensure proper conduct and avoid mis-selling"], 3, "AMCs review the processes and conduct of distributors that meet set thresholds, including suitability checks and handling of conflicts, to protect investors."], ["Equity Futures and Forwards", "A stock trades at ₹2,000. The risk-free rate is 7% a year and a dividend of ₹10 is expected before expiry in 3 months. Using simple interest, the fair futures price is:", ["₹2,035, since dividends do not affect futures prices", "₹2,140", "₹1,965", "₹2,025"], 3, "Fair value = 2,000 × (1 + 0.07 × 0.25) − 10 = 2,035 − 10 = ₹2,025. Expected dividends reduce the cost of carry because the futures holder does not receive them."], ["Fund Distribution and Channel Management", "Which practice is acceptable for a mutual fund distributor?", ["Promising guaranteed returns on long-short strategies to attract high net worth clients", "Collecting investor cheques in the distributor's own name", "Disclosing commissions received", "Signing forms on clients' behalf"], 2, "Distributors must disclose commissions for schemes they recommend. Promising returns, collecting money in their own name or signing for clients are prohibited."], ["Legal and Regulatory Framework", "All schemes of a mutual fund together may not hold more than what share of the voting capital of any single company?", ["10%", "25% of the paid-up capital of each investee company", "5%", "51%"], 0, "The 10% fund-level cap on voting rights prevents mutual funds from gaining control of investee companies and limits concentration."], ["NAV, TER and Pricing of Units", "A segregated portfolio is created in a debt scheme when:", ["A held security suffers a credit event", "The scheme's AUM falls below ₹100 crore during any quarter of the financial year", "The fund manager resigns", "Interest rates rise sharply"], 0, "Segregation (side-pocketing) separates a downgraded or defaulted security from the main portfolio so new and redeeming investors are treated fairly, and existing investors keep any later recovery."], ["Exchange Traded Interest Rate Options", "A debt fund wants protection against rising yields but wishes to keep gains if yields fall. The suitable option position is to:", ["Sell call options on bond futures to earn premium and fully remove the risk", "Buy calls", "Buy puts on bond futures", "Sell puts"], 2, "Long puts gain when bond prices fall (yields rise), protecting the portfolio, while upside from falling yields is retained, less the premium paid."], ["Risk, Return and Performance of Funds", "Compared with a fully invested long-only equity fund, a long-short fund with lower net exposure will typically have:", ["Lower sensitivity to market moves", "Higher beta and larger swings than the market in both directions", "No risk of any loss", "Zero tracking error"], 0, "Lower net exposure means less market (beta) risk. Losses are still possible if stock selection on either side goes wrong."], ["Understanding Index", "Index futures are widely used to hedge diversified equity portfolios because:", ["Index futures eliminate both systematic and stock-specific risk completely", "Index futures need no margin", "Portfolios largely move with the index", "Index futures never expire"], 2, "A diversified portfolio's risk is mostly market risk, which index futures can offset. Stock-specific risk remains."], ["Investment Landscape", "An investor feels the pain of a ₹1 lakh loss far more than the pleasure of a ₹1 lakh gain and therefore refuses to sell losing positions. This reflects:", ["Overconfidence in one's own stock-picking ability and research", "Anchoring", "Loss aversion", "Home bias"], 2, "Loss aversion means losses hurt more than equal gains please. It often leads investors to hold losers too long in the hope of breaking even."], ["Fund Distribution and Channel Management", "An investor who wants to invest in an SIF through a distributor will be allotted units of the:", ["Direct plan, because SIFs have only one plan for all investors", "Regular plan", "Institutional plan", "Segregated plan"], 1, "Investments through distributors go into the regular plan, whose expenses include distributor commission. Investments without a distributor go into the direct plan."], ["Taxation", "A resident investor receives IDCW from a mutual fund. Which statement is correct?", ["It is exempt because the fund has already paid dividend distribution tax on it", "It is taxed at the investor's slab rate", "It is taxed at 12.5% flat", "It is taxed only on redemption"], 1, "Dividend distribution tax was abolished in 2020. IDCW is now taxed in the investor's hands at slab rates, with TDS above the prescribed threshold."], ["NAV, TER and Pricing of Units", "Daily accrual of expenses means that:", ["Expenses are charged to investors once a year as a separate bill payable by cheque", "NAV is published net of expenses", "Expenses are deducted from SIP instalments only", "Expenses are recovered from distributors"], 1, "Expenses are accrued every day within the TER, so each published NAV already reflects them. Investors do not pay a separate bill."], ["Strategies Using Equity Futures and Options", "A ₹1.5 crore portfolio has a beta of 1.1. Each index futures contract is worth ₹15 lakh. To fully hedge the portfolio, the manager should sell:", ["10 contracts, ignoring beta, since the portfolio is diversified", "11 contracts", "15 contracts", "9 contracts"], 1, "Contracts = portfolio value × beta / contract value = 1.5 crore × 1.1 / 15 lakh = 11. Ignoring beta gives 10, leaving the higher-beta portfolio under-hedged."], ["Strategies Using Interest Rate Futures and Options", "A trader expects the yield curve to steepen (long yields rising more than short yields). A futures trade that fits this view is to:", ["Buy long-tenor futures and sell short-tenor futures in equal duration amounts", "Buy both tenors", "Sell long, buy short tenor futures", "Sell only short-tenor futures"], 2, "If long yields rise more, long-tenor futures fall more. Selling them and buying short-tenor futures profits from the steepening with limited exposure to parallel moves."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Performance", "Equity scheme returns are compared with a Total Return Index rather than a price index because:", ["TRI excludes dividends, making the comparison stricter for the fund", "Price indices are not published", "TRI includes dividends", "TRI is calculated by the AMC"], 2, "Funds receive dividends and include them in NAV, so the fair comparison is with a TRI, which assumes dividends are reinvested."], ["Risk, Return and Performance of Funds", "Which risk measure focuses only on returns below a minimum acceptable level?", ["Downside deviation", "Standard deviation, which treats upside and downside moves in the same way", "Beta", "R-squared"], 0, "Downside deviation measures volatility of returns below a target. It is used in the Sortino ratio, which penalises only harmful volatility."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Selection", "An investor who wants daily liquidity with very low risk should prefer:", ["A sector rotation long-short SIF with a weekly redemption window and notice period", "A small cap fund", "A liquid or overnight fund", "A credit risk fund"], 2, "Liquid and overnight funds offer daily redemption and low volatility. SIFs may restrict redemptions and carry higher risk."], ["Strategies Using Equity Futures and Options", "A trader buys an ₹800 call for ₹15 and an ₹800 put for ₹12. At expiry, the stock must be beyond which levels for the trade to make a profit?", ["Above ₹815 or below ₹788, using only each option's own premium", "Above ₹827 or below ₹773", "Between ₹773 and ₹827", "Exactly at ₹800"], 1, "A long straddle costs ₹27 in total. It breaks even at 800 + 27 = ₹827 and 800 − 27 = ₹773, and profits only if the price moves beyond these levels."], ["Understanding Index", "A Total Return Index differs from a Price Return Index because it:", ["Excludes all dividends and corporate actions from the index calculation", "Assumes dividends are reinvested", "Uses only bank stocks", "Is calculated once a year"], 1, "TRI adds back dividends as if reinvested, so it rises more than the price index over time and is the fair benchmark for funds."], ["Exchange Traded Interest Rate Options", "A borrower buys a cap at 8% and sells a floor at 6%. This combination is called a:", ["Straddle, which profits from large rate moves in either direction", "Collar", "Swaption", "Calendar spread"], 1, "A collar limits the borrowing cost between 6% and 8%. Selling the floor reduces the cost of the cap but gives up benefits below 6%."], ["Strategies Using Equity Futures and Options", "An investor holds shares and sells call options on them to earn extra income in a flat market. This strategy is a:", ["Covered call", "Protective put, which limits downside by buying the right to sell", "Long straddle", "Bear put spread"], 0, "A covered call earns premium against existing shares. It caps upside above the strike but adds income when prices stay range-bound."], ["Exchange Traded Interest Rate Futures", "Why are IRF positions marked to market daily?", ["To settle gains and losses each day", "To let traders avoid paying any losses until the contract finally expires", "To fix the interest rate", "To change the lot size"], 0, "Daily MTM settles each day's change in value, so losses are paid as they occur. This reduces credit risk for the clearing corporation and participants."], ["Equity Options", "The maximum loss for the writer of a naked call option is:", ["Unlimited", "Limited to the premium received at the time of writing the call", "Equal to the strike price", "Zero"], 0, "If the stock rises without limit, the call writer must deliver at the strike, facing theoretically unlimited loss. Only the premium is the maximum gain."], ["Taxation", "Securities Transaction Tax applies on redemption of units of:", ["Debt-oriented schemes and liquid funds, at the same rate as equity schemes", "All schemes equally", "Gold ETFs only", "Equity-oriented schemes"], 3, "STT is levied on redemption of units of equity-oriented funds, at a small rate. Redemption of debt schemes does not attract STT."], ["Basics of Derivatives", "Which of the following is an example of an arbitrage?", ["Buying call options because one expects a sharp rally in a stock", "Holding shares for ten years", "Selling futures to hedge a portfolio", "Buying shares in cash and selling overpriced futures"], 3, "Arbitrage locks in a nearly riskless profit from mispricing, such as buying in the cash market and selling relatively overpriced futures of the same stock."], ["Legal and Regulatory Framework", "A distributor tells a client that an SIF's long-short approach 'makes losses impossible'. This statement is:", ["Correct, since shorts offset all long positions under every market condition", "Correct for hybrid strategies only", "Acceptable if the client is wealthy", "Misleading and a breach of conduct"], 3, "Long-short strategies can still lose money, for example when both long and short positions move against the manager. Promising no losses is misrepresentation."], ["Strategies Using Equity Futures and Options", "An investor wants to keep a stock but protect against a sharp fall, while retaining upside. The suitable strategy is to:", ["Sell a call on the stock, which earns premium and fully removes downside risk", "Sell a put", "Buy a put", "Buy more stock"], 2, "A protective put acts like insurance: losses below the strike are offset by the put, while gains above remain, net of the premium paid."], ["Interest Rate Derivatives", "In a plain vanilla interest rate swap, the payments exchanged on each date are normally:", ["Exchanged in full, including the notional principal, on every payment date", "Paid only by the fixed payer", "Deferred until maturity", "Netted"], 3, "Both legs are in the same currency, so only the net difference between fixed and floating interest is paid by the party owing more."], ["Interest Rates and Fixed Income Market", "Positive convexity of a bond means that:", ["Price falls faster when yields fall than it rises when yields rise", "Price gains exceed losses for equal yield moves", "The bond has no duration", "The bond pays no coupon"], 1, "Convexity makes the price–yield curve bowed. For an equal fall and rise in yield, the gain is larger than the loss, which benefits holders."], ["Investment Landscape", "Which investor is most likely to need a high allocation to liquid, low-risk assets?", ["A 30-year-old saving for retirement through a monthly SIP over thirty years", "One with a 15-year goal and stable income", "One with surplus wealth and no near-term needs", "One with a large expense due in three months"], 3, "Money needed soon should not be exposed to market swings. Long horizons and stable incomes allow more equity; near-term needs call for liquidity and safety."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Selection", "For an investor who needs ₹30,000 a month from a ₹50 lakh corpus with moderate risk, a better structure than IDCW is:", ["The IDCW option of a small cap fund, since IDCW is always fixed", "A single lumpsum redemption", "An SWP from a hybrid scheme", "An ELSS with lock-in"], 2, "An SWP gives a fixed, predictable cash flow, and only the gain portion of each withdrawal is taxed. IDCW amounts are not assured and are fully taxable."], ["Understanding Index", "Why do index providers periodically rebalance indices?", ["To guarantee that the index rises every year", "To keep the index representative", "To raise brokerage income for exchanges", "To fix the prices of constituent stocks"], 1, "Rebalancing replaces stocks that no longer meet criteria and adjusts weights, so the index continues to represent its intended market segment."], ["Equity Options", "A call with strike ₹500 when the stock is at ₹470 is:", ["In the money, because the stock is close to the strike price of the option", "Out of the money", "At the money", "Deep in the money"], 1, "A call is out of the money when the underlying price is below the strike. Exercising it now would mean paying ₹500 for a stock worth ₹470."], ["Interest Rate Derivatives", "An FRA has a contract rate of 7% on ₹10 crore for 6 months (0.5 year). The reference rate is set at 7.4%. Ignoring discounting, the payment to the FRA buyer is:", ["₹4,00,000, using the full year instead of half a year", "₹2,00,000", "₹20,000", "₹74,00,000"], 1, "Payment = 10 crore × (7.4% − 7%) × 0.5 = ₹2,00,000. The buyer gains when the reference rate exceeds the contract rate."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Selection", "Which investor is the most suitable for an equity long-short SIF?", ["A wealthy, experienced investor with a long horizon", "A first-time investor who needs all of the money back within six months", "A retiree relying on the corpus for monthly expenses", "A student investing pocket money"], 0, "SIFs suit investors who can meet the minimum, understand derivative-based strategies, can bear losses and do not need quick access to the money."], ["Interest Rate Derivatives", "A bank expects to lend money for 6 months starting 3 months from now and fears rates will fall. To lock in its lending rate, it should:", ["Buy an FRA, which gains when interest rates rise above the agreed rate", "Pay fixed in a swap", "Sell bond futures", "Sell an FRA"], 3, "A lender gains from higher rates. Selling an FRA (receiving the fixed contract rate) protects against falling rates, as the FRA pays out if the reference rate falls below the contract rate."], ["Risk, Return and Performance of Funds", "A fund holding thinly traded securities faces heavy redemptions. The danger it faces is best described as:", ["Sponsor risk, i.e. the sponsor being unable to support the AMC financially", "Interest rate risk", "Tracking risk", "Liquidity risk"], 3, "Illiquid holdings may have to be sold at a discount to meet redemptions, which hurts remaining investors. Notice periods in SIFs help manage this."], ["Taxation", "An investor switches units from an equity long-short SIF strategy to a debt strategy of the same AMC. For tax purposes, this is:", ["Exempt, since the AMC and the investor remain the same", "Taxable only if exit load is charged", "A taxable transfer", "Treated as a gift"], 2, "A switch is a redemption from one scheme or strategy and a purchase in another, so any capital gain on the units switched out is taxable."], ["Understanding Index", "An equal-weighted index of 50 stocks will, compared with its market-cap-weighted version, typically:", ["Give exactly the same weight to each stock as the market-cap-weighted version", "Have no volatility", "Contain only large banks", "Give more weight to smaller companies"], 3, "Equal weighting gives every stock 2%, so smaller companies get relatively more weight than in a cap-weighted index, often increasing volatility."], ["Interest Rates and Fixed Income Market", "A nominal rate of 8% a year compounded quarterly is equivalent to an effective annual rate of about:", ["8.00%, as compounding frequency does not change the annual rate", "8.16%", "8.24%", "8.30%"], 2, "Effective rate = (1 + 0.08/4)^4 − 1 = 1.02^4 − 1 = 8.24%. More frequent compounding raises the effective rate above the nominal rate."], ["Equity Futures and Forwards", "A trader sells 2 lots of stock futures (lot size 500) at ₹1,200. The settlement price that day is ₹1,185. The MTM result is:", ["Gain of ₹15,000", "Loss of ₹15,000, because the price has moved down", "Gain of ₹7,500", "Loss of ₹7,500"], 0, "Short position gains when price falls: 2 × 500 × (1,200 − 1,185) = ₹15,000. ₹7,500 counts only one lot."], ["Concept and Role of a Mutual Fund", "A Hybrid Long-Short SIF must hold at least:", ["65% in equity and the rest in gold", "25% each in equity and in debt", "80% in debt and money market instruments, with no equity at all", "50% in REITs and InvITs"], 1, "The hybrid long-short strategy invests at least 25% in equity and at least 25% in debt, with flexibility for the rest and limited unhedged shorts."], ["Legal and Regulatory Framework", "A mutual fund scheme may borrow only to meet temporary liquidity needs. The maximum borrowing is:", ["50% of net assets, for up to one year, to buy additional securities", "Unlimited, with trustee approval", "20% of net assets, up to 6 months", "10% of net assets for any purpose"], 2, "Borrowing is allowed only for redemptions or IDCW payouts, up to 20% of net assets and for a maximum of six months. It cannot be used to leverage the portfolio."], ["Equity Options", "A stock trades at ₹1,000. A call with strike ₹1,000 costs ₹30. Over the option's life, the risk-free return is 2%. By put-call parity, a put with the same strike should cost about:", ["₹30.00, because ATM calls and puts always have the same price", "₹49.61", "₹10.39", "₹19.61"], 2, "Parity: C + PV(K) = P + S. PV(K) = 1,000 / 1.02 = 980.39. P = 30 + 980.39 − 1,000 = ₹10.39."], ["Strategies Using Equity Futures and Options", "A trader buys a ₹100 strike call for ₹6 and sells a ₹110 strike call for ₹2 on the same stock and expiry. The maximum profit is:", ["₹10, the full difference between the two strike prices", "₹4", "₹8", "₹6"], 3, "Net cost = 6 − 2 = ₹4. Maximum profit = strike difference − net cost = 10 − 4 = ₹6, reached when the stock is at or above ₹110 at expiry."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Selection", "Holding several long-short SIFs with similar strategies mainly creates:", ["Automatic hedging that removes all risk from the overall portfolio", "Overlap with little diversification", "Lower total minimum investment", "A tax exemption"], 1, "Similar strategies tend to hold similar positions, so adding more of them increases cost and complexity without much diversification benefit."], ["Exchange Traded Interest Rate Options", "An interest rate floor is most useful for:", ["Borrowers with floating-rate loans who fear that rates may rise sharply", "Equity investors", "Exporters", "Floating-rate lenders"], 3, "A floor pays out when rates fall below the floor rate, protecting an investor or lender who earns floating-rate interest against falling income."], ["Interest Rates and Fixed Income Market", "When market interest rates fall, the price of an existing fixed-coupon bond:", ["Falls, because its coupon now looks unattractive compared with new bonds", "Stays the same", "Rises", "Becomes zero"], 2, "Existing bonds with higher coupons become more valuable when new yields fall, so their prices rise. Prices and yields move inversely."], ["Exchange Traded Interest Rate Futures", "A bond portfolio has a PVBP of ₹30,000. An interest rate futures contract has a PVBP of ₹150. The number of contracts needed to hedge is:", ["200", "150, matching the PVBP of one futures contract", "300", "450"], 0, "Hedge ratio = portfolio PVBP / futures PVBP = 30,000 / 150 = 200 contracts. Selling these would offset most of the portfolio's sensitivity to a 1 bp move."], ["Equity Options", "Exercise of a Nifty option bought on an Indian exchange is possible:", ["On any trading day up to and including the expiry date, at the holder's choice", "Only on the purchase date", "Never", "Only at expiry"], 3, "European options may be exercised only at expiry. All exchange-traded equity options in India are European style."], ["Risk, Return and Performance of Funds", "A debt portfolio has a modified duration of 3. If yields rise by 0.5%, its value will change by approximately:", ["+1.5%, as rising yields increase the coupon income of the portfolio", "−1.5%", "−3.0%", "−0.5%"], 1, "Price change ≈ −modified duration × change in yield = −3 × 0.5% = −1.5%. Prices fall when yields rise."], ["NAV, TER and Pricing of Units", "An investor redeems 2,000 units at an NAV of ₹45 with an exit load of 0.5%. The amount paid before tax is:", ["₹90,450, because the exit load is added to the NAV paid to the investor", "₹90,000", "₹89,550", "₹89,100"], 2, "Redemption price = 45 × (1 − 0.005) = ₹44.775. Proceeds = 2,000 × 44.775 = ₹89,550. ₹89,100 applies a 1% load instead."], ["Investment Landscape", "Two assets have a correlation of −0.3. Combining them in a portfolio will most likely:", ["Double the portfolio's expected return", "Reduce overall portfolio volatility", "Remove all market risk from the portfolio, whatever the weights chosen", "Increase volatility compared with each asset alone"], 1, "Assets that do not move together offset part of each other's fluctuations, so portfolio volatility falls below the weighted average. Negative correlation does not remove all risk or double returns."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Selection", "Why may an index fund be preferred by some investors over an actively managed large cap fund?", ["Index funds guarantee to beat the market by a fixed margin each year", "Index funds have no market risk", "Index funds carry a lock-in", "Lower cost and no manager risk"], 3, "Index funds track the market at low cost and avoid the risk of active underperformance. They still carry full market risk."], ["Investor Services", "An NRI wants returns on a mutual fund investment to be fully repatriable. The investment should be funded from:", ["An NRO account, which is always fully repatriable without limits", "A resident savings account", "An NRE account", "Cash"], 2, "NRE account funds and proceeds are freely repatriable. NRO funds are generally non-repatriable, subject to permitted remittance limits."], ["Concept and Role of a Mutual Fund", "The key feature that distinguishes an SIF from a regular equity mutual fund is that an SIF:", ["Guarantees a minimum return to investors who stay invested for three years or more", "Has no minimum investment", "Is not regulated by SEBI", "Can take limited short positions"], 3, "SIFs bridge mutual funds and PMS/AIFs by allowing long-short strategies with a higher minimum ticket. Regular mutual funds cannot take unhedged short positions."], ["Fund Distribution and Channel Management", "A client with a total investable corpus of ₹12 lakh wants to put ₹10 lakh in a long-short SIF. The distributor's best response is to:", ["Point out the concentration risk", "Process it immediately, since the minimum is met and the client has asked for it", "Ask for ₹2 lakh more", "Suggest borrowing to invest more"], 0, "Meeting the minimum does not make a product suitable. Putting over 80% of savings in a complex strategy creates concentration risk; the distributor should assess suitability carefully."], ["Interest Rate Derivatives", "A debt long-short SIF wants to benefit if yields rise. Using interest rate futures, it would:", ["Buy interest rate futures, which rise in price when yields go up", "Buy long-duration bonds", "Sell interest rate futures", "Hold cash only"], 2, "Bond futures prices fall when yields rise, so a short futures position gains. This lets a debt long-short strategy express a view on rising rates."], ["Taxation", "Equity-oriented fund units held for 10 months are sold at a gain of ₹80,000. Ignoring cess, the tax on this gain is:", ["₹10,000, since gains below ₹1.25 lakh are taxed at the long-term rate", "₹12,000", "₹16,000", "Nil"], 2, "Units held for 12 months or less give short-term gains, taxed at 20% for equity-oriented funds. 20% of ₹80,000 = ₹16,000. The ₹1.25 lakh exemption applies only to long-term gains."], ["Understanding Index", "In a free-float market capitalisation index, a company's weight depends on:", ["Total number of employees of the company and its subsidiaries", "Face value of its shares", "Market value of shares available to the public", "Its share price alone"], 2, "Free-float methodology counts only shares freely available for trading, excluding promoter and locked-in holdings, and multiplies by price to set weights."], ["Equity Futures and Forwards", "An investor fears a short-term fall in a stock he holds but does not want to sell it. Using futures, he can:", ["Buy more futures on the same stock to average his cost", "Buy index call options", "Do nothing", "Sell futures on that stock"], 3, "Selling futures creates an offsetting short position: if the stock falls, gains on the futures offset losses on the holding."], ["Exchange Traded Interest Rate Futures", "An insurer expects a large inflow in two months to invest in G-secs and fears yields will fall. It can lock in today's yields by:", ["Selling IRF now and buying the bonds later with the inflow", "Buying IRF now", "Selling bonds short", "Buying a cap"], 1, "If yields fall, bond prices rise. Long futures gain in that case, offsetting the higher price the insurer will pay for bonds later."], ["Understanding Index", "An index has a base value of 1,000. The current market capitalisation of its constituents is 25 times the base period capitalisation. The index level is:", ["25,000", "1,025, adding 25 to the base value", "40", "2,500"], 0, "Index = base value × current market cap / base market cap = 1,000 × 25 = 25,000."], ["Exchange Traded Interest Rate Futures", "A debt fund expects yields to rise and wants to cut portfolio duration without selling bonds. It should:", ["Buy IRF, which increases duration when rates move", "Buy longer bonds", "Do nothing", "Sell IRF"], 3, "Short futures carry negative duration, so selling them lowers the portfolio's overall duration and reduces losses if yields rise."], ["Scheme Related Information", "Portfolio disclosure for SIF strategies helps investors mainly to:", ["Trade the underlying stocks themselves on the same day at the scheme's cost price", "Check holdings against the mandate", "Avoid exit loads", "Calculate their tax liability exactly"], 1, "Periodic disclosure shows what the strategy actually holds, including derivative positions, so investors can confirm it follows its stated approach."], ["Exchange Traded Interest Rate Futures", "A trader buys 4 IRF contracts (face value ₹2 lakh, quoted per ₹100) at 97.80. The next day's settlement price is 97.55. The MTM is:", ["Gain of ₹2,000, because lower prices mean higher yields", "Loss of ₹2,000", "Loss of ₹20,000", "Loss of ₹200"], 1, "MTM = 4 × 2,00,000 × (97.55 − 97.80) / 100 = −₹2,000. A buyer loses when the futures price falls."], ["Exchange Traded Interest Rate Options", "A put option on bond futures has a strike of 98.00. The futures price at expiry is 96.75. The intrinsic value is:", ["0.00, as the put is out of the money at this futures price", "96.75", "2.50", "1.25"], 3, "Put intrinsic value = strike − underlying = 98.00 − 96.75 = 1.25. The put is in the money because the futures price is below the strike."], ["Exchange Traded Interest Rate Options", "A buyer pays a premium of 0.80 for a put on bond futures with strike 99.00. The break-even futures price at expiry is:", ["98.20", "99.80, adding the premium to the strike price", "99.00", "97.40"], 0, "A put buyer breaks even when the underlying is at strike minus premium: 99.00 − 0.80 = 98.20. Below that level the position is profitable."], ["Strategies Using Equity Futures and Options", "A trader expects a stock to stay in a narrow range until expiry and is willing to take large risk if it moves sharply. Which strategy profits most from this view?", ["Long strangle, buying an out-of-the-money call and an out-of-the-money put", "Long call", "Long futures", "Short straddle"], 3, "A short straddle (selling an ATM call and put) earns maximum premium if the stock stays near the strike, but has large losses if it moves sharply either way."], ["Legal and Regulatory Framework", "An investor's SIF holding falls below ₹10 lakh only because the market fell. Under SEBI's framework, this is treated as:", ["A passive breach, not requiring exit", "A violation that requires the AMC to redeem all units immediately at the prevailing NAV", "A reason to charge a penalty", "Grounds to freeze the folio"], 0, "The minimum investment is tested on investment amount. A fall in value due to market movement is a passive breach and does not force redemption."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Performance", "A high tracking error in an index fund indicates that:", ["The fund has beaten the index by a large margin in every single period", "The fund has low expenses", "The index is volatile", "Returns deviate a lot from the index"], 3, "Tracking error is the variability of the gap between fund and index returns. A passive fund should keep it low; a high figure points to poor replication."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Performance", "When comparing SIF strategies, a fair comparison would be between:", ["Strategies of the same type", "An equity long-short SIF and a liquid fund, using one-year returns only", "An SIF and a bank FD", "Any two SIFs regardless of type"], 0, "Strategy types differ in risk and objective. Like-for-like comparison within the same strategy type, and against an appropriate benchmark, is meaningful."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Selection", "An investor wants equity exposure with lower downside than a long-only fund, can invest ₹25 lakh, and has a 5-year horizon. A reasonable option to consider is:", ["An overnight fund, as it gives equity returns with no downside", "A gilt fund with 10-year maturity", "A sectoral long-only fund", "An equity long-short SIF"], 3, "Equity long-short strategies aim to participate in equity markets while using shorts to soften drawdowns. The investor's ticket size and horizon fit this product."], ["Exchange Traded Interest Rate Options", "Time value of an option is greatest when the option is:", ["At the money", "Deep in the money, where it behaves almost exactly like the underlying itself", "Expired", "Deep out of the money"], 0, "At-the-money options have the most uncertainty about finishing in the money, so they carry the highest time value. Deep in or out of the money options have less."], ["Legal Structure of Mutual Funds", "Which entity maintains the record of unitholders and processes their transactions for most AMCs?", ["The sponsor of the mutual fund, through its own branch network", "The custodian", "The RTA", "The statutory auditor"], 2, "Registrar and transfer agents handle investor records, transaction processing, statements and many investor service requests on behalf of the AMC."], ["NAV, TER and Pricing of Units", "Which expense must be borne by the AMC rather than charged to the scheme?", ["Custodian fees charged for safekeeping of the scheme's securities within the limit", "Expenses above the TER limit", "Registrar fees", "Audit fees"], 1, "Only permitted expenses up to SEBI's TER limits can be charged to a scheme. Anything beyond the limit is borne by the AMC, its sponsor or trustees."], ["Interest Rates and Fixed Income Market", "For the same bond (2 years, 10% annual coupon, 8% yield), the Macaulay duration is about:", ["2.00 years, because duration always equals the remaining maturity", "1.91 years", "1.50 years", "1.09 years"], 1, "Duration = (1 × 9.26 + 2 × 94.31) / 103.57 ≈ 1.91 years. For coupon bonds, duration is below maturity; it equals maturity only for zero-coupon bonds."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Performance", "For investments made at different dates and amounts, such as a SIP, the most appropriate return measure is:", ["Simple absolute return calculated from the first instalment date to today", "Point-to-point CAGR", "Average NAV", "XIRR"], 3, "XIRR accounts for the timing and size of each cash flow, giving an annualised return for irregular or periodic investments like SIPs."], ["Equity Futures and Forwards", "Basis is defined as spot price minus futures price. As expiry approaches, basis generally:", ["Widens steadily, reaching its maximum on the expiry day itself", "Converges to zero", "Stays constant", "Becomes equal to the dividend"], 1, "Futures and spot prices converge at expiry because the futures then represent immediate delivery. Any gap would be arbitraged away."], ["NAV, TER and Pricing of Units", "A scheme declares an IDCW of ₹2 per unit when its cum-IDCW NAV is ₹32. Ignoring market movement, the ex-IDCW NAV is:", ["₹32, as IDCW is paid out of the AMC's own profits and not from the NAV", "₹34", "₹30", "₹31"], 2, "IDCW is paid out of the scheme's assets, so NAV falls by the amount paid: 32 − 2 = ₹30. The investor's total value (NAV plus cash) is unchanged."], ["Scheme Related Information", "Why might an SIF strategy have a notice period for redemptions while most regular open-ended equity schemes do not?", ["Because SEBI bans redemptions from SIFs during the first three years after the launch", "To earn extra fees from investors", "Because SIF NAVs are declared yearly", "To manage less liquid long-short positions"], 3, "Long-short and concentrated strategies may need time to unwind positions in an orderly way. A notice period protects remaining investors from forced selling."], ["Interest Rate Derivatives", "The main purpose of an interest rate cap for a floating-rate borrower is to:", ["Set a maximum borrowing rate", "Guarantee that the borrower always pays exactly the floating market rate", "Remove principal risk", "Increase the loan size"], 0, "A cap pays the borrower when the reference rate exceeds the cap rate, effectively placing a ceiling on interest cost while keeping the benefit of falling rates."], ["Equity Options", "Which factor increases the premium of both call and put options, other things equal?", ["A rise in the underlying stock price, which raises both calls and puts together", "Shorter time to expiry", "Higher volatility", "Lower interest rates"], 2, "Higher volatility raises the chance of large moves, which benefits option holders on both sides. A price rise increases calls but reduces puts."], ["NAV, TER and Pricing of Units", "Exit load collected by a scheme is:", ["Credited back to the scheme", "Retained by the distributor as an additional commission for the investor's account", "Paid to SEBI", "Kept by the AMC as profit"], 0, "Exit load, net of GST, is credited to the scheme, so it benefits remaining investors and discourages short-term flows that impose costs on them."], ["Strategies Using Equity Futures and Options", "In a bear put spread, the trader:", ["Buys a lower-strike call and sells a higher-strike call to profit from a rising market", "Sells two puts", "Buys a higher-strike put and sells a lower-strike put", "Buys a call and a put at the same strike"], 2, "A bear put spread profits from a moderate fall. Selling the lower-strike put reduces cost but caps the gain at the strike difference minus net premium."], ["Exchange Traded Interest Rate Futures", "If a G-sec's coupon income exceeds the repo financing rate, its theoretical futures price will be:", ["Above the spot price by the amount of coupon income received", "Equal to face value", "Zero", "Below spot"], 3, "Futures = spot + financing cost − coupon income. When coupon income is larger, carry is positive and futures trade below spot."], ["Basics of Derivatives", "Counterparty risk in exchange-traded futures is largely removed by:", ["Allowing each buyer to pick a seller they personally know and trust", "Avoiding margins", "The clearing corporation acting as central counterparty", "Daily changes in lot size"], 2, "Through novation, the clearing corporation becomes buyer to every seller and seller to every buyer, backed by margins and default funds."], ["Risk, Return and Performance of Funds", "A long-short strategy is 90% long and 30% short. Its net market exposure is:", ["120%, being the total of the long and the short positions added together", "90%", "60%", "30%"], 2, "Net exposure = long − short = 90% − 30% = 60%. Gross exposure (long + short) is 120%, which indicates total positions rather than market sensitivity."], ["Scheme Related Information", "Which of these would be a change in a fundamental attribute requiring an exit option for existing investors?", ["Appointment of a new co-fund manager for the strategy alongside the existing one", "Change in the AMC's head office address", "Change in investment objective", "Revision of the factsheet design"], 2, "Investment objective, type of scheme and terms of issue are fundamental attributes. Changing them requires notice and an exit option at NAV without load."], ["Basics of Derivatives", "A forward contract differs from a futures contract mainly because a forward is:", ["Customised and traded OTC", "Standardised, exchange-traded and settled daily through the clearing corporation", "Always cash-settled", "Free from all risk"], 0, "Forwards are private bilateral agreements with terms set by the parties and carry counterparty risk. Futures are standardised, exchange-traded and marked to market daily."], ["Understanding Index", "In a price-weighted index, which stock has the greatest influence on the index?", ["The highest-priced stock", "The company with the largest market capitalisation, regardless of price", "The smallest company", "The most traded stock"], 0, "Price-weighted indices give more weight to higher-priced shares, regardless of company size. This is why most modern indices use market capitalisation instead."], ["Legal Structure of Mutual Funds", "The Investment Management Agreement of a mutual fund is signed between:", ["Sponsor and investors, at the time of every new fund offer of a scheme", "Trustees and the AMC", "AMC and distributors", "Custodian and SEBI"], 1, "The IMA appoints the AMC to manage the fund's money and sets out its duties. It is executed between the trustees and the AMC."], ["Strategies Using Interest Rate Futures and Options", "To hedge a bond portfolio with a modified duration of 4 using futures with a modified duration of 8 and equal values, the hedge ratio (futures value to portfolio value) is:", ["2.0, the futures duration divided by the portfolio duration", "1.0", "0.5", "4.0"], 2, "Hedge ratio = portfolio duration / futures duration = 4 / 8 = 0.5. Selling futures worth half the portfolio's value offsets its rate sensitivity."], ["Equity Options", "An option's delta of 0.6 means that its premium will change by about:", ["₹6 for every ₹1 move in the underlying, as delta is quoted in tens", "₹1 for every ₹0.6 move in time", "Nothing", "₹0.60 for a ₹1 move"], 3, "Delta measures sensitivity to the underlying price. A delta of 0.6 implies about ₹0.60 change in premium for each ₹1 change in the underlying."], ["Basics of Derivatives", "An SIF fund manager holds a stock and sells futures on the same stock in equal quantity. The combined position has:", ["Near-zero net exposure to that stock", "Double the exposure to the stock's price movements, both up and down", "Unlimited upside", "No margin requirement"], 0, "The short futures offset the long stock, so price moves largely cancel out. Such hedged positions do not count towards the unhedged short limit."], ["Investor Services", "For an equity scheme, a purchase application received at 2:45 pm on Wednesday, with funds reaching the scheme's account at 4 pm the same day, gets:", ["Wednesday's NAV, because the application itself was received before the 3 pm cut-off", "Tuesday's NAV", "Friday's NAV", "Thursday's NAV"], 3, "For equity schemes, the applicable NAV depends on when funds are available for use. Funds realised after the 3 pm cut-off get the next business day's NAV."], ["Investment Landscape", "Inflation is 6% and a debt investment yields 7% before tax. For an investor in the 30% tax slab, the approximate real post-tax return is:", ["+1.0%", "+4.9%, since tax applies only to the real part of the return", "−1.1%", "+0.1%"], 2, "Post-tax yield = 7% × (1 − 0.30) = 4.9%. Subtracting 6% inflation gives about −1.1%. Taxes apply to the full nominal return, which is why high-slab investors can lose purchasing power."], ["Taxation", "A long-term capital loss on mutual fund units that cannot be set off this year can be carried forward for up to:", ["4 assessment years, and only against business income of those years", "1 year", "Indefinitely", "8 assessment years"], 3, "Capital losses can be carried forward for eight assessment years, provided the return is filed on time. Long-term losses can only be set off against long-term gains."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Selection", "Before recommending an SIF, a distributor should particularly confirm that the investor:", ["Has invested in at least three new fund offers in the last year", "Understands derivatives risks", "Has a demat account with CDSL", "Is below 40 years of age"], 1, "SIFs use derivatives and short positions, which can behave unexpectedly. Understanding and accepting these risks is essential to suitability."], ["Strategies Using Interest Rate Futures and Options", "IRF are trading above fair value based on cost of carry. The arbitrage is to:", ["Sell the bond short and buy the overpriced futures contract", "Buy futures only", "Sell both", "Buy the bond, sell futures"], 3, "Cash-and-carry: buy the bond, fund it in the repo market, and sell the overpriced futures. Convergence at expiry locks in the mispricing."], ["Exchange Traded Interest Rate Options", "A call option on a bond futures contract will gain value if:", ["Interest rates rise, reducing the bond futures price at expiry", "Interest rates fall", "The option nears expiry", "Volatility falls"], 1, "Lower rates increase bond prices, so a call on bond futures moves into the money. Rising rates benefit puts instead."], ["Equity Options", "A put option with strike ₹800 is bought for a premium of ₹25. At expiry the stock is at ₹740. The buyer's net profit per share is:", ["₹35", "₹60, since the premium was already paid earlier and is a sunk cost", "₹25", "₹85"], 0, "Payoff = 800 − 740 = ₹60. Net profit = 60 − 25 = ₹35 per share. ₹85 wrongly adds the premium."], ["Investor Services", "A Consolidated Account Statement for a demat account holder who also holds mutual fund units in statement form is generated by:", ["The depositories", "The investor's bank, along with the monthly savings bank statement", "SEBI", "Each distributor separately"], 0, "Where the investor has a demat account, the depositories send a single CAS covering demat holdings and mutual fund folios linked through PAN."], ["Legal and Regulatory Framework", "An AMC's SIF advertisement uses the same brand and look as its regular mutual fund schemes. Under SEBI norms this is:", ["Allowed, as long as the SIF has a separate fund manager", "Allowed for accredited investors only", "Required for brand consistency", "Not allowed"], 3, "SIFs must carry distinct branding so investors do not confuse them with regular mutual fund schemes that have lower minimums and different risks."], ["Investor Services", "An investor already holds ₹12 lakh across SIF strategies of an AMC and wants to invest ₹1 lakh in another strategy of the same AMC. Under the minimum investment rule:", ["Not allowed, since every new strategy needs a separate ₹10 lakh investment", "This is allowed", "Allowed only through a lumpsum of ₹5 lakh", "Allowed only for accredited investors"], 1, "The ₹10 lakh minimum is checked at the investor's PAN level across all SIF strategies of the AMC. Having met it, the investor can add smaller amounts."], ["Interest Rate Derivatives", "The overnight MIBOR in India is mainly used as the benchmark for:", ["Overnight indexed swaps", "Equity index futures traded on the National Stock Exchange", "Home loan EMIs", "Gold ETFs"], 0, "OIS contracts in India are commonly benchmarked to overnight MIBOR. They let participants exchange fixed rates for compounded overnight rates."], ["Legal Structure of Mutual Funds", "Who is responsible for ensuring that an AMC launching SIF strategies complies with SEBI regulations and protects investors' interests?", ["The distributors who sell the strategy and collect application forms from investors", "The stock exchange", "The trustees", "The investors' bankers"], 2, "Trustees oversee the AMC's activities, including SIF strategies, and must ensure compliance and fair treatment of investors."], ["Basics of Derivatives", "Which is TRUE of an option buyer compared with an option seller?", ["The buyer must post large margins because the buyer's loss is unlimited", "The seller has limited loss", "The buyer's loss is limited to the premium", "Both have identical risk"], 2, "An option buyer pays a premium and can lose at most that amount. The seller receives the premium but faces potentially large losses, hence margins."]]};
